WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.361
Welcome to Part 2 of Cloud DNS
on AWS, Azure and Oracle Cloud,

2
00:00:04.361 --> 00:00:09.750
a Federal Modernization Workbook
evaluation that's still under review.

3
00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:12.062
Part 1 covered the native resolvers.

4
00:00:12.062 --> 00:00:16.524
Part 2 asks when an agency should
run its own DNS servers in a cloud,

5
00:00:16.524 --> 00:00:21.400
and how to run BIND there so it stays
inside Book 08's single policy plane.

6
00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:23.716
We'll start with the test that matters:

7
00:00:23.716 --> 00:00:27.525
does a cloud BIND server align
with that plane, or create a silo?

8
00:00:27.525 --> 00:00:34.062
Then the criteria, vNIOS or BIND, and BIND
itself, from roles to patching and Git.

9
00:00:34.062 --> 00:00:36.995
Then a seven-question
checklist for an agency

10
00:00:36.995 --> 00:00:40.237
adding BIND servers in AWS,
and the federal mapping.

11
00:00:40.237 --> 00:00:45.920
The labels carry over from Part 1:
design guidance, inference, and confirm.

12
00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:55.123
Additional BIND servers in AWS align
with Book 08 when they are forwarders or

13
00:00:55.123 --> 00:01:00.832
resolvers under central configuration,
logged to the SOC, owned and with a reason.

14
00:01:00.832 --> 00:01:05.671
BIND aligns when it's a resolver or
forwarder whose configuration comes from

15
00:01:05.671 --> 00:01:10.433
the policy plane: its forwarding, its
addresses and its logging all governed.

16
00:01:10.433 --> 00:01:14.557
It aligns as a deliberate bridge,
with an owner and an end date.

17
00:01:14.557 --> 00:01:18.795
It aligns when Git configures it, and
when it's hardened and inventoried.

18
00:01:18.795 --> 00:01:23.735
It creates a silo when it hosts zones
authored apart from the DDI platform,

19
00:01:23.735 --> 00:01:28.755
when it's edited by hand, when it skips
the shared Protective DNS, validation

20
00:01:28.755 --> 00:01:34.220
and logging, or when it duplicates the
Route 53 Resolver for no stated reason.

21
00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:42.108
When should an agency run its own
DNS servers in a cloud at all?

22
00:01:42.108 --> 00:01:46.375
Microsoft's guidance is direct:
use DNS Private Resolver

23
00:01:46.375 --> 00:01:51.096
instead of custom DNS server
software like BIND when possible.

24
00:01:51.096 --> 00:01:55.808
AWS steers hybrid DNS to
Resolver endpoints too.

25
00:01:55.808 --> 00:01:58.445
So the criteria, as design guidance.

26
00:01:58.445 --> 00:02:01.214
Run your own when a zone
must be authoritative

27
00:02:01.214 --> 00:02:03.545
in the cloud and reached by delegation.

28
00:02:03.545 --> 00:02:06.401
When the agency's response policy zones,

29
00:02:06.401 --> 00:02:11.854
or its encrypted-DNS logging at the
agency resolver, must apply exactly as on

30
00:02:11.854 --> 00:02:17.307
premises and the native filter can't carry
them, or, as on Oracle, there isn't one.

31
00:02:17.307 --> 00:02:21.958
When workloads need encrypted DNS
the native endpoints don't offer.

32
00:02:21.958 --> 00:02:24.208
For a migration bridge with an end date.

33
00:02:24.208 --> 00:02:26.996
Or when load would exceed
the per-instance quotas.

34
00:02:26.996 --> 00:02:29.920
Otherwise, stay with the native resolver.

35
00:02:33.670 --> 00:02:36.933
If the agency does run its
own servers, which ones?

36
00:02:36.933 --> 00:02:42.407
Where its DNS runs on an Infoblox
Grid, vNIOS is the natural choice.

37
00:02:42.407 --> 00:02:46.332
It joins the same Grid and takes
its policy from the Grid Master.

38
00:02:46.332 --> 00:02:51.908
One caution: vNIOS failover in GovCloud
and Azure Government is a point to confirm.

39
00:02:51.908 --> 00:02:54.364
BIND is the choice when there's no Grid,

40
00:02:54.364 --> 00:02:58.008
for a small footprint, or when
a tool expects BIND itself.

41
00:02:58.008 --> 00:03:01.614
BIND has no policy plane
of its own; for Book 08,

42
00:03:01.614 --> 00:03:05.319
its policy comes from
the DDI platform and Git.

43
00:03:05.319 --> 00:03:06.308
Which BIND?

44
00:03:06.308 --> 00:03:10.333
ISC supports each even-numbered
branch for four years.

45
00:03:10.333 --> 00:03:16.137
As of October twenty-ninth, 9.20 is
the supported production branch,

46
00:03:16.137 --> 00:03:21.520
with end of life in 2028, and 9.18
reached end of life in June 2026.

47
00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:26.860
Operating-system packages follow their
vendor, so record which support you rely on.

48
00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:35.397
BIND does three jobs, and NIST and
ISC both say to keep them apart.

49
00:03:35.397 --> 00:03:40.760
NIST says an internet-facing server shouldn't
serve in both roles, and ISC agrees.

50
00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:43.898
In a cloud, the forwarder
is the common case.

51
00:03:43.898 --> 00:03:47.786
It holds no zones; it applies
agency policy and logging,

52
00:03:47.786 --> 00:03:50.810
then forwards each name
to the right place.

53
00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:56.051
A recursive resolver fits where agency
policy and logging must apply at the first

54
00:03:56.051 --> 00:04:01.060
hop, with the same response policy zones,
validation and logging as on premises.

55
00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:05.962
And authoritative service fits only for
a delegated zone the native services

56
00:04:05.962 --> 00:04:10.110
can't answer, or as a secondary of
a zone mastered on the DDI platform.

57
00:04:10.110 --> 00:04:12.760
Never a separately authored primary.

58
00:04:16.510 --> 00:04:17.822
Where do the servers go?

59
00:04:17.822 --> 00:04:22.986
As design guidance: in the shared-services
or network hub VPC or virtual network

60
00:04:22.986 --> 00:04:28.148
that already holds the native resolver's
endpoints, never in workload accounts.

61
00:04:28.148 --> 00:04:29.998
Each cloud adds a constraint.

62
00:04:29.998 --> 00:04:36.159
On AWS, a BIND server can forward only
to its own VPC's plus-two address.

63
00:04:36.159 --> 00:04:42.435
On Azure, a custom DNS server sits
in the same virtual network as the

64
00:04:42.435 --> 00:04:48.710
VMs whose hostnames it resolves,
and forwards those to 168.63.129.16.

65
00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:53.709
On Oracle Cloud, it forwards the VCN's own

66
00:04:53.709 --> 00:04:58.860
names and private views
to 169.254.169.254.

67
00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:01.714
For high availability,
NIST asks for at least two

68
00:05:01.714 --> 00:05:05.210
servers on different segments,
dispersed geographically.

69
00:05:05.210 --> 00:05:08.727
In practice: two or more per
Region in different zones,

70
00:05:08.727 --> 00:05:13.090
a second Region, and every client
configured with all of them.

71
00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:18.215
How does BIND forward?

72
00:05:18.215 --> 00:05:22.303
The native resolver stays the only
path to the cloud's own names.

73
00:05:22.303 --> 00:05:24.853
BIND's forward setting defaults to first,

74
00:05:24.853 --> 00:05:28.528
which falls back to its own
recursion when forwarders fail.

75
00:05:28.528 --> 00:05:33.283
For private names, that fallback can
return the public answer or NXDOMAIN,

76
00:05:33.283 --> 00:05:37.415
so the AWS whitepaper says it's
critical to specify forward only.

77
00:05:37.415 --> 00:05:39.792
Per-instance limits: on AWS,

78
00:05:39.792 --> 00:05:46.709
the 1,024-packet-per-second quota covers
everything BIND sends to VPC plus two;

79
00:05:46.709 --> 00:05:50.707
on Azure, a VM may send
1,000 queries per second,

80
00:05:50.707 --> 00:05:54.165
and queries over the limit are dropped.

81
00:05:54.165 --> 00:05:57.527
Size the fleet so no server
forwards near the limit.

82
00:05:57.527 --> 00:06:01.377
Visibility: the VPC Resolver
sees BIND as the source,

83
00:06:01.377 --> 00:06:04.689
never the client, so BIND's
logs are the record.

84
00:06:04.689 --> 00:06:07.544
And loops: don't forward
a domain to an inbound

85
00:06:07.544 --> 00:06:10.920
endpoint from inside the
network that holds it.

86
00:06:14.670 --> 00:06:18.758
Here's the forwarder role on
AWS, from the evaluation.

87
00:06:18.758 --> 00:06:20.070
It's illustrative.

88
00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:25.027
It passed BIND's configuration
checker on version 9.18.39

89
00:06:25.027 --> 00:06:29.457
but it hasn't run against a
VPC or been checked on 9.20.

90
00:06:29.457 --> 00:06:33.148
And in practice it's rendered
from the policy plane and Git,

91
00:06:33.148 --> 00:06:34.608
never typed on the host.

92
00:06:34.608 --> 00:06:38.384
Version none hides the version,
queries and recursion are

93
00:06:38.384 --> 00:06:41.845
limited to the agency's
clients, and transfers are off.

94
00:06:41.845 --> 00:06:46.300
Validation is on, with an exceptions
list for the private namespaces,

95
00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:48.020
which have no DNSSEC chain.

96
00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:50.803
Internet-bound queries go forward only,

97
00:06:50.803 --> 00:06:55.358
to the agency resolvers, the
governed upstream to Protective DNS.

98
00:06:55.358 --> 00:06:59.070
The cloud's names go to this
VPC's own plus-two resolver,

99
00:06:59.070 --> 00:07:02.445
and on-premises names go
to the agency resolvers.

100
00:07:02.445 --> 00:07:09.689
On Oracle Cloud and Azure,
the same file sends those

101
00:07:09.689 --> 00:07:16.430
clouds' names to 169.254.169.254
or 168.63.129.16.

102
00:07:20.180 --> 00:07:21.742
DNSSEC has two halves.

103
00:07:21.742 --> 00:07:23.531
BIND validates by default.

104
00:07:23.531 --> 00:07:27.159
But behind the VPC Resolver,
BIND can't validate:

105
00:07:27.159 --> 00:07:32.743
the VPC Resolver ignores the DNSSEC
OK bit and returns no DNSSEC records.

106
00:07:32.743 --> 00:07:35.064
So validate in the VPC Resolver,

107
00:07:35.064 --> 00:07:39.081
or in BIND toward an upstream
that returns DNSSEC data.

108
00:07:39.081 --> 00:07:41.439
Oracle's VCN resolver validates,

109
00:07:41.439 --> 00:07:46.418
but whether it passes that data back
to BIND isn't documented; confirm.

110
00:07:46.418 --> 00:07:50.566
Private zones under a signed parent
go on BIND's exceptions list,

111
00:07:50.566 --> 00:07:52.131
held in the policy plane.

112
00:07:52.131 --> 00:07:55.561
And whether Protective
DNS returns DNSSEC records

113
00:07:55.561 --> 00:07:58.593
to forwarders is a point
to confirm with CISA.

114
00:07:58.593 --> 00:08:01.480
Signing: no cloud signs private zones.

115
00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:06.170
NIST says signing internal zones is
not recommended except where required,

116
00:08:06.170 --> 00:08:08.255
and notes agencies may require it.

117
00:08:08.255 --> 00:08:12.666
The design guidance is that the
zone's one writable owner signs it,

118
00:08:12.666 --> 00:08:18.080
with keys on the Grid Master or an HSM,
so a cloud BIND secondary holds no keys.

119
00:08:21.830 --> 00:08:24.368
Response policy zones are BIND's filter.

120
00:08:24.368 --> 00:08:28.113
They're ordinary DNS zones,
up to 64 per server,

121
00:08:28.113 --> 00:08:32.467
whose triggers map to actions
like NXDOMAIN or drop.

122
00:08:32.467 --> 00:08:34.981
They stay in sync through zone transfers.

123
00:08:34.981 --> 00:08:40.030
CISA's Protective DNS Resolver is
mandatory for civilian agencies.

124
00:08:40.030 --> 00:08:45.103
CISA says agency DNS should use only
Protective DNS as its upstream,

125
00:08:45.103 --> 00:08:51.155
and cloud infrastructure should use
Protective DNS or agency DNS as its upstream.

126
00:08:51.155 --> 00:08:54.462
CISA's pages don't mention
response policy zones,

127
00:08:54.462 --> 00:08:57.205
so the layering is NIST's
and the program's.

128
00:08:57.205 --> 00:09:02.050
As design guidance: the agency's
own policy zones run on cloud BIND,

129
00:09:02.050 --> 00:09:05.818
from the same feed as on
premises, in front of CISA's.

130
00:09:05.818 --> 00:09:10.001
Internet-bound queries leave
only toward Protective DNS.

131
00:09:10.001 --> 00:09:12.006
Hits and blocks go to the SOC.

132
00:09:12.006 --> 00:09:16.716
And BIND 9.20 can forward
over DNS over TLS;

133
00:09:16.716 --> 00:09:22.060
CISA's 2024 note that it
can't was true of 9.18.

134
00:09:25.810 --> 00:09:29.510
Logging is where cloud
DNS most often goes dark.

135
00:09:29.510 --> 00:09:35.660
NIST asks for robust DNS traffic logging,
with blocked queries always logged.

136
00:09:35.660 --> 00:09:42.641
OMB's M-22-09 expects agencies to keep
visibility by logging at their designated

137
00:09:42.641 --> 00:09:45.634
resolvers, and M-26-14 sets the retention:

138
00:09:45.634 --> 00:09:49.623
searchable for six months
and retrievable for a year.

139
00:09:49.623 --> 00:09:51.636
BIND offers two mechanisms.

140
00:09:51.636 --> 00:09:55.639
The logging statement writes
categories such as queries,

141
00:09:55.639 --> 00:09:59.059
responses and policy-zone
hits to files or syslog,

142
00:09:59.059 --> 00:10:04.397
and dnstap records messages to a file or
socket, in a build with dnstap enabled.

143
00:10:04.397 --> 00:10:09.060
ISC warns that query logging adds
heavy overhead, so plan for it.

144
00:10:09.060 --> 00:10:12.560
Send them to the SIEM,
where ASIM's DNS parser

145
00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:16.060
already handles BIND,
as Book 12 describes.

146
00:10:16.060 --> 00:10:19.782
Because the native resolver sees
BIND rather than the client,

147
00:10:19.782 --> 00:10:21.898
BIND's log is the record of who asked.

148
00:10:21.898 --> 00:10:25.460
Without it, cloud DNS is a blind spot.

149
00:10:29.210 --> 00:10:33.135
The patching sources are ISC's,
and the clock is CISA's.

150
00:10:33.135 --> 00:10:36.176
Subscribe to ISC's
bind-announce list and check

151
00:10:36.176 --> 00:10:39.372
its vulnerability matrix
for each version in use.

152
00:10:39.372 --> 00:10:44.862
And patch to CISA's clock: Binding
Operational Directive 26-04, from June 2026,

153
00:10:44.862 --> 00:10:49.923
replaced the older remediation
directives with risk-based timelines,

154
00:10:49.923 --> 00:10:55.498
the shortest three days for the highest-risk
flaws in publicly exposed assets.

155
00:10:55.498 --> 00:10:57.584
Version none hides the version.

156
00:10:57.584 --> 00:11:01.025
Query access defaults to
all hosts, so restrict it,

157
00:11:01.025 --> 00:11:04.298
and restrict recursion
to the agency's clients.

158
00:11:04.298 --> 00:11:08.636
Restrict zone transfers and
authenticate them with TSIG keys.

159
00:11:08.636 --> 00:11:13.360
Run named unprivileged, and
keep the host to DNS only.

160
00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:20.334
For a baseline, CIS has benchmarks
only for BIND 9.11 and 9.9,

161
00:11:20.334 --> 00:11:22.560
none for 9.20 or 9.18.

162
00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:28.670
So the agency states its own profile,
approved by the CISO, as design guidance.

163
00:11:32.420 --> 00:11:35.145
Who owns a cloud BIND
server's configuration?

164
00:11:35.145 --> 00:11:38.158
The workbook's Ansible
guide manages such Linux

165
00:11:38.158 --> 00:11:41.020
hosts by playbook from
a reviewed repository.

166
00:11:41.020 --> 00:11:44.158
Four practices make it
work, as design guidance.

167
00:11:44.158 --> 00:11:47.962
Rendered, not typed: BIND's
configuration is a template

168
00:11:47.962 --> 00:11:51.345
rendered from data the
DDI policy plane exports.

169
00:11:51.345 --> 00:11:56.305
Checked before it lands: every change
arrives by reviewed pull request,

170
00:11:56.305 --> 00:11:59.583
and CI and the playbook
both run BIND's checkers.

171
00:11:59.583 --> 00:12:04.068
Git is the evidence: the commit
and tag are the CM-2 baseline,

172
00:12:04.068 --> 00:12:09.770
the pull request is the CM-3 change
record, and check-mode runs report drift.

173
00:12:09.770 --> 00:12:15.545
One owning tool: Ansible owns named.conf on
these hosts, and nothing else writes it.

174
00:12:15.545 --> 00:12:18.120
A hand edit is drift, to be reverted.

175
00:12:18.120 --> 00:12:19.785
And secrets stay out of Git:

176
00:12:19.785 --> 00:12:24.010
keys and certificates come from
the secrets store at run time.

177
00:12:27.760 --> 00:12:31.435
Here's the checklist, seven
questions an agency asks

178
00:12:31.435 --> 00:12:35.110
of each BIND server it adds
in AWS, as design guidance.

179
00:12:35.110 --> 00:12:40.610
First establish whether they're EC2
instances or VMware guests on AWS.

180
00:12:40.610 --> 00:12:46.635
One, role: a forwarder, resolver or
secondary aligns; a primary doesn't.

181
00:12:46.635 --> 00:12:52.285
Two, source of truth: no zones, or
zones transferred from the DDI platform.

182
00:12:52.285 --> 00:12:57.860
Three, why BIND: a stated need from the
criteria, or a bridge with an end date.

183
00:12:57.860 --> 00:13:00.009
Four, configuration: rendered,

184
00:13:00.009 --> 00:13:04.135
reviewed and applied by
automation, not edited on the host.

185
00:13:04.135 --> 00:13:07.599
Five, placement: the
hub VPC of each Region,

186
00:13:07.599 --> 00:13:10.860
at least two servers, rules shared once.

187
00:13:10.860 --> 00:13:15.942
Six, shared policy: logs in the
SIEM with the M-26-14 retention,

188
00:13:15.942 --> 00:13:21.660
the same policy-zone feed, and
Protective DNS as the only upstream.

189
00:13:21.660 --> 00:13:27.822
Seven: a supported branch, a stated
baseline, a CMDB record and a named owner.

190
00:13:27.822 --> 00:13:30.659
Rows two and four are the serious signals.

191
00:13:30.659 --> 00:13:34.780
Row three alone means the
servers may not be needed at all.

192
00:13:38.530 --> 00:13:43.567
The federal mapping reuses the
controls Book 08 has already verified.

193
00:13:43.567 --> 00:13:48.756
It's illustrative, so validate it against
the system's own authorization boundary.

194
00:13:48.756 --> 00:13:51.330
Configuration and inventory first.

195
00:13:51.330 --> 00:13:54.508
CM-2, the baseline:
the evidence is the Git

196
00:13:54.508 --> 00:13:58.168
commit and tag of the
rendered configuration.

197
00:13:58.168 --> 00:14:03.043
CM-3, change control: pull
requests with required review.

198
00:14:03.043 --> 00:14:09.642
CM-6, configuration settings: the agency's
stated profile, plus drift reports.

199
00:14:09.642 --> 00:14:14.963
CM-7, least functionality: a dedicated
DNS host, one role per server,

200
00:14:14.963 --> 00:14:19.593
the version hidden, and
recursion and transfers limited.

201
00:14:19.593 --> 00:14:23.444
CM-8, inventory: a CMDB
record for each server

202
00:14:23.444 --> 00:14:27.830
and an IPAM entry for each
address, with an owner.

203
00:14:27.830 --> 00:14:31.795
And SI-2, flaw remediation:
a supported BIND branch,

204
00:14:31.795 --> 00:14:35.856
ISC's announcement list
and vulnerability matrix,

205
00:14:35.856 --> 00:14:42.500
and remediation inside the timelines of
Binding Operational Directive 26-04.

206
00:14:46.250 --> 00:14:49.570
Then resolution, logging
and Protective DNS.

207
00:14:49.570 --> 00:14:54.683
SC-20 asks for signed authoritative
data: the zone's one owner signs it,

208
00:14:54.683 --> 00:14:58.900
and cloud secondaries serve
signed data and hold no keys.

209
00:14:58.900 --> 00:15:05.151
SC-21 asks for validation: validation on,
the exceptions list from the policy plane,

210
00:15:05.151 --> 00:15:09.287
and VPC Resolver validation
where BIND forwards to it.

211
00:15:09.287 --> 00:15:14.524
SC-22: at least two servers
per Region, roles separated.

212
00:15:14.524 --> 00:15:20.363
SI-4 treats DNS as a detection source,
with every filter's hits in the SIEM.

213
00:15:20.363 --> 00:15:27.138
AU-2 and AU-12: BIND's logs beside
the three clouds' resolver logs,

214
00:15:27.138 --> 00:15:30.326
kept for the M-26-14 retention.

215
00:15:30.326 --> 00:15:37.781
For M-22-09's encrypted DNS and resolver
logging: encrypted DNS to clients,

216
00:15:37.781 --> 00:15:43.137
DNS over TLS upstream from BIND
9.20, and logs at the BIND server.

217
00:15:43.137 --> 00:15:47.981
CISA's guidance: internet-bound
forwarding only to Protective

218
00:15:47.981 --> 00:15:52.001
DNS or agency resolvers, and
other DNS egress blocked.

219
00:15:52.001 --> 00:15:58.880
And Emergency Directive 19-01 is sunset,
but its four actions remain sound practice.

220
00:16:02.630 --> 00:16:04.230
Where does Infoblox fit?

221
00:16:04.230 --> 00:16:08.674
Where the Grid already runs the
agency's DNS, vNIOS is the choice,

222
00:16:08.674 --> 00:16:13.630
because it joins the same Grid and
gets its policy from the Grid Master.

223
00:16:13.630 --> 00:16:16.092
The same criteria apply as for BIND.

224
00:16:16.092 --> 00:16:19.592
Confirm its failover in
GovCloud and Azure Government.

225
00:16:19.592 --> 00:16:21.560
The open points for this part:

226
00:16:21.560 --> 00:16:26.481
whether Oracle's VCN resolver passes
DNSSEC data to a resolver behind it,

227
00:16:26.481 --> 00:16:31.729
and whether CISA's Protective DNS
returns DNSSEC data to agency forwarders.

228
00:16:31.729 --> 00:16:35.805
And the illustrative configuration
hasn't yet run in a VPC.

229
00:16:35.805 --> 00:16:40.132
The next steps: ask the seven
questions of every cloud BIND server,

230
00:16:40.132 --> 00:16:44.779
build the forwarder pattern in a test
account in each Region and realm,

231
00:16:44.779 --> 00:16:48.305
and run Book 08's validation
matrix from every origin.

232
00:16:48.305 --> 00:16:53.230
This is research under review, not an
endorsement by any federal agency.

233
00:16:53.230 --> 00:16:54.060
Thank you.
